WEBVTT

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.874
Two ways to make a bench ESD-safe.

00:00:05.154 --> 00:00:06.004
Both work.

00:00:06.284 --> 00:00:07.663
So which one should you choose?

00:00:08.263 --> 00:00:13.863
Many teams set up electronics benches, then later need them for other work, or the reverse.

00:00:14.143 --> 00:00:18.643
The ESD choice you make today affects how flexible that bench stays.

00:00:19.243 --> 00:00:26.179
Option one: an electrostatic laminate worktop, called PELW, with ESD accessories.

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:31.644
It is built into the bench, ideal when the station will always be used for electronics.

00:00:31.924 --> 00:00:38.090
Option two: a standard laminate worktop with a 2 millimetre ESD mat and accessories.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.979
It costs less, and the bench can return to general use later.

00:00:42.259 --> 00:00:46.247
Either way, the wrist strap and grounding connection are what make it work.

00:00:46.847 --> 00:00:50.889
One aerospace R&D team we worked with uses both:

00:00:50.889 --> 00:00:55.269
Flexi workstations with PELW tops for PCB debugging,

00:00:55.269 --> 00:00:59.648
and a Base bench with an ESD mat in the coding area.

00:01:00.248 --> 00:01:01.990
Permanent electronics station?

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:03.835
Choose PELW.

00:01:04.115 --> 00:01:05.349
Want flexibility?

00:01:05.629 --> 00:01:06.580
Choose the mat.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:08.019
Which suits your lab?

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:13.038
Comment below, and next, see exactly how an ESD kit connects you to earth.

00:01:13.638 --> 00:01:17.883
Furnisys: complete workspace systems, planned around your process.

